                        kind @note
                        text "node_by_path (src/graph.rs:97), BuildLog.entries (src/log.rs:27), and the depfile dedup maps (src/deps.rs:127-149) all pay SipHash via std RandomState. The comment at graph.rs:95 cites collision-attack protection, but the adversary would be the manifest ronin is about to execute arbitrary commands from — hashDoS is outside that threat model, and both ninja and C samurai use fixed hashes. Swap to the in-tree rapidhash (src/htab.rs) as a BuildHasher; if the hardening property is still wanted, seed rapidhash randomly, which keeps it at a fraction of SipHash cost."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-memchr-scanner
                title "Scan manifests with memchr runs and lazy line accounting"
                deps (ronin-alloc-metrics)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T08:10:56.120155796Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "The lexer steps one byte at a time through current()/next() (src/scan.rs:134-136, 190-199), paying per byte: a bounds-checked get, an Option construction, a newline test, and line/column bookkeeping — samurai's getc-style scanner transliterated, minus the raw-pointer walk. Two changes: (1) drop per-byte line/column tracking — spans already carry byte offsets, so compute line/column lazily on error by counting newlines in the prefix; (2) skip literal runs with memchr-class scans (bstr already provides them) — in scanstring (scan.rs:424-455) the interesting byte set is tiny ($, :, |, space, tab, CR, LF), so jump to the next interesting byte instead of stepping. This is the moral equivalent of ninja's re2c lexer and drives the wide no-op (19.3 vs 9.1 ms) and path-canonicalization (10.4 vs 5.6 ms) rows in benchmarks/baseline-2026-07-29.md."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-path-scratch
                title "Reuse path evaluation scratch and canonicalize in place"
                deps (ronin-memchr-scanner)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T08:11:05.054232878Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Every path occurrence in parseedge allocates at least twice even when the node already exists: evaluated_path builds a fresh BString (src/parse.rs:134-145), then canonpath allocates a new output Vec and a components Vec per call (src/util.rs:133-170), and only then does mknode hit the intern map. scanstring additionally allocates a Vec<ScannedEvalPart> per path and scanpaths collects a Vec of those Vecs (src/scan.rs:424-467) though most paths are a single literal part. Proposals: give ScannedEvalString an inline single-part fast path or evaluate each path immediately with a reused parts scratch; evaluate into a reusable scratch buffer; canonicalize in place with component offsets in a small stack array (as ninja and samurai both do). End state: an already-interned path reference costs zero allocations."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-path-interning-arena
                title "Store node paths once behind an interning table and byte arena"
                deps (ronin-fast-hashing)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T08:11:13.985885763Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Every node path is stored three times: Node.path, an eagerly computed Node.shellpath that copies even in the no-quote case covering ~99% of real paths (shell_escape_path, src/graph.rs:505 — C samurai shares the pointer there), and a third copy as the node_by_path HashMap key (graph.rs:193). Steps: make shellpath Option<BString> (None = same bytes as path) or lazy; eliminate the key copy with an interning table storing only NodeIds that hashes/compares against graph.nodes[id].path (hashbrown::HashTable expresses this directly; std HashMap's owned-key requirement is why the to_vec exists). Endgame: a single append-only byte arena for all path text with nodes holding (u32 offset, u32 len) spans — contiguous, cache-friendly, allocation-free per node, and it makes deps-log loading nearly free (see ronin-log-span-loading). This is the n2 interner design and composes with the span-frontend direction."
                    }
                )
            }

